const body = `<h1>9.2b — Override vs Overload: Know the Difference</h1>

<div class="callout warn">
    <strong>Season 2: beyond the exam.</strong> This topic is no longer tested
    (the 2025 CED removed inheritance from the exam). We build it after May 12
    because it makes the RPG engine dramatically better, and because you are
    ready for it. Pure craft, zero test anxiety.
</div>



<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Basic Application</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h3>Build: Weapon System</h3>
        <p>Override changes behavior in a subclass (same signature). Overload provides multiple versions of the same method name (different parameters). These are easily confused — you'll build both.</p>
        <p>Create <code>Weapon</code> (parent):</p>
        <ol>
            <li>Field: <code>String name</code>, <code>int baseDamage</code>.</li>
            <li>Method: <code>int getDamage()</code> — returns baseDamage.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Create <code>EnchantedWeapon extends Weapon</code>:</p>
        <ol>
            <li>Additional field: <code>int bonusDamage</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Override</strong> <code>getDamage()</code> — returns baseDamage + bonusDamage. Mark it with <code>@Override</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Overload</strong> <code>getDamage(String element)</code> — if element matches the weapon's element, add 5 more. Use <code>@Override</code>? Why or why not?</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Test both. Call <code>getDamage()</code> and <code>getDamage("fire")</code> on an EnchantedWeapon reference. Now store it in a Weapon variable — which methods are accessible?</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-09/overriding.html">Method Overriding</a></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-09/extends-and-super.html">Inheritance</a></li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
